# Fixing client Report tasks

Applies whenever the user asks to fix issues from the Report chat / client reports
(e.g. "report check karke fix karo", "report ke issues fix karo").
The task list is `client-reports/TASKS.md`; screenshots are in `client-reports/media/`.

## Before the loop
1. Run `npm run report-inbox` in `apps/backend` (read-only) to pull new client messages.
2. Triage any entry still showing `Type: _to triage_`: open its screenshots, fill Type and Notes.

## Status labels
Every task heading starts with its status: `[Not started]`, `[In progress]`, `[Complete]`, `[Blocked]`,
`[Fix failed]`, `[Waiting for client]`, `[Client replied]` or `[Awaiting apply]` (Auto-fix finished it in its copy;
it becomes Complete only when Apply fix puts the change in the main files, or Fix failed when it is discarded). Always set it with the commands below (in `apps/backend`), never by hand:
`npm run report-inbox -- --mark <in-progress|complete|blocked|failed> <messageId> [<messageId>...]`
It updates the heading and the status overview. `in-progress` and `complete` also put the status on that
message in the live Report chat ("Developer · In progress" / "Developer · Complete" on localhost).
Pass the task's id together with its "Part of: …" entries. Only mark messages you actually work on.
The user never sets labels.

## Waiting for the client
- A task needs something only the client can give (information, a file, a screenshot, a decision):
  `npm run report-inbox -- --ask <messageId> --need "<what is needed>" --question "<one short polite question>"`.
  It asks the client as a reply to their message, shows In progress on it in the live chat and sets `[Waiting for client]`.
  Never ask the client for passwords, API keys or other secrets: mark those `blocked` instead.
- The task message alone is clear: fix it directly, ask nothing. Not clear: read the 2 tasks before and after it.
  Still unclear: `npm run report-inbox -- --unclear <messageId> --need "<what is unclear>" --options "a | b" --from <developer|client>`
  sets `[Needs input]` and shows a form under Auto-fix; the developer's answer sets `[Input given]` (fixed first, like Client replied).
- A waiting task never blocks the rest: after asking, go on with the next pending task.
- When the client replies to the question, the inbox check folds the reply into the task as a collapsed
  "Client reply" block and sets `[Client replied]`: that task is ready to fix and comes first.
- A requirement found anywhere in the history: `npm run report-inbox -- --attach <taskMessageId> <foundMessageId>`
  adds it collapsed under the task with its details and marks the found message's own task "Part of" it.
  `npm run report-inbox -- --history` prints every message of every chat for searching (reads only).

## The loop — strictly one task at a time, never batch fixes
1. Pick in this strict order:
   a) a `[Client replied]` task (its requirement is fulfilled), fix it;
   b) else each `[Waiting for client]` task, oldest first: if the client answered or the requirement is found anywhere
      in the history, attach it (`--attach`) and fix that task;
   c) else (nothing answered or found) leave them waiting, do not ask again, and fix the first `[Not started]` entry
      after the last `[Complete]` one (none after it: the first `[Not started]` from the top).
   Mark the picked task `in-progress`.
2. Fix only that task, following omsking-qa: failing test first, smallest fix, no unrelated changes.
3. Verify: re-run the new test plus the affected suites (and the admin build for UI changes);
   for UI work also check the screen locally in the browser. Live integrations: mocks only.
4. Verification passed → mark it `complete` with `--issue "<issue kya tha>" --fix "<fix kya kiya>"`: one short
   Hinglish line each (Hindi words in English letters, not Devanagari). They are also saved on the live update, so the
   hover shows on the chat chip and the side list module on the live site too. Also pass
   `--check "<page kholo; kya dabao; kya dikhna chahiye>"`: 2-4 short Hinglish steps to verify the fix, shown under the
   message's open-page icon. Then add
   `- Status: Fixed and verified <date> — <what was checked>; local only, not deployed`.
5. Verification failed → retry once with a different approach. Still failing → undo your own changes for
   this task, mark it `failed`, add `- Status: <date> — <reason>`, and move on. The live chat keeps showing
   In progress (the chat has no way back to unmarked); tell the user that.
6. Needs something from the client → `--ask` (see Waiting for the client) and move on to the next pending task.
   Needs something else you can't get (secrets, a live action) → mark it `blocked` before starting (so the chat
   stays unmarked), add `- Status: Blocked — <what is needed>`, move on.
7. Give the user a one-line update after each task, then continue with the next one.

## Never without the user asking
Deploying, committing or pushing, replying to the client (other than the `--ask` question for a missing
requirement), or any live chat change other than the In progress / Complete marks above.
The watcher appends new tasks and only edits a task to fold in a client reply to its question.
